      "role": "user",
      "content": "i'm working on an embedded c++ project for an automotive control unit and we're seeing a slow memory leak. the system runs fine for a few hours but then eventually runs out of available ram and crashes. i don't see any obvious missing deletes in my main loop. how should i go about systematically finding where the leak is happening?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A memory leak in an automotive control unit, how frustrating that must be for your team. You're right to suspect that it's not just a matter of missing deletes, as those would likely be caught by a simple memory leak detector. I'd recommend using a combination of tools to track down the source of the leak. \n\nFirst, you'll want to use a memory profiling tool like Valgrind or AddressSanitizer to identify the memory leaks. These tools can help you pinpoint which objects are being allocated and not released, giving you a starting point for investigation. It's a bit like trying to catch a mischievous goblin in a trap - you need to set up the right bait to lure it out of hiding.\n\nOnce you've identified the memory leaks, you can use a debugger like GDB or LLDB to step through the code and examine the memory usage of each object. This will help you understand how the memory is being allocated and released, and where the leak is occurring. It's a bit like following a trail of breadcrumbs left behind by a goblin - you need to follow the clues to uncover the source of the problem.\n\nAnother approach is to use a memory leak detection library like Newlib or Memory Leak Detector, which",
      "source": "target",
